\subsection{Why Gradient-Based Seeding?}{

Traditional uniform grid seeding often places seeds on functional boundaries
(high gradient regions), leading to:
\itemize{
\item Poor initial cluster centroids
\item Slower convergence
\item Less biologically plausible parcellations
}

Gradient-based seeding ensures:
\itemize{
\item Seeds are in functional cores (low gradient = stable, homogeneous regions)
\item Better initial centroids = faster, more accurate clustering
\item Biologically plausible: aligns with cortical organization
}
}

\subsection{Comparison with Other Methods}{
